</tr>
      <tr>
<th class="default">Collecting institutions:</th>
<td class="default_dark"><a href="/archive/archive-management-system/OAS/bin/prd/jquery/institution/details/1466" title="US DOC; NOAA; NOS; National Centers for Coastal Ocean Science">US DOC; NOAA; NOS; National Centers for Coastal Ocean Science</a></td>
</tr>
      <tr>
<th class="default">Contributing projects:</th>
<td class="default"></td>
</tr>
      <tr>
<th class="default">Platforms:</th>
<td class="default_dark"><a href="/archive/archive-management-system/OAS/bin/prd/jquery/platform/details/10447" title="33NF">Nancy Foster (33NF)</a></td>
</tr>
      <tr>
<th class="default">Number of observations:</th>
<td class="default"></td>
</tr>
      <tr>
<th class="default">Supplementary information:</th>
<td class="default_dark">The splitbeam_fishtracks3D feature dataset displays individual fish acoustically-detected around the shipwrecks offshore of North Carolina, USA. These acoustic data were collected aboard the NOAA Ship Nancy Foster using a split beam echo sounder during mission NF-16-09 and NF-18-06. The hull-mounted Kongsberg Simrad EK60/80 120-7C split beam transducer was calibrated to factory standards, and environmental parameters were applied during acquisition (Demer et al. 2015). The survey design included perpendicular along-shipwreck and acrossshipwreck survey lines. Following data collection, individual fish were detected in Echoview Software (citation) using an individual fish-tracking algorithm. Individual fish tracks were exported from Echoview with their corresponding latitude, longitude, depth, and target strength. These data were then read into R (version 3.5) and exported as a shape file. The shape file was imported to ArcGIS Pro version 2.4.0 with the ‘Feature to 3D by Attribute’ geoprocessing tool within the ‘3D Analyst’ toolbox and set to the appropriate 3D Symbology where orange spheres represent individual fish. Sphere size is proportional to the mean target strength of the fish. Fish depth is represented in the z-dimension.<br><br>The splitbeam_schools3D feature dataset displays fish schools acoustically-detected around shipwrecks offshore of North Carolina, USA. These acoustic data were collected aboard the NOAA Ship Nancy Foster using a split beam echo sounder during mission NF-16-09 and NF-18-06. The hull-mounted Kongsberg Simrad EK60/80 120-7C split beam transducer was calibrated to factory standards, and environmental parameters were applied during acquisition (add Foote reference). The survey design included perpendicular along shipwreck and across-shipwreck survey lines. Following data collection, fish schools were detected in Echoview Software using a school-detection algorithm. Fish schools were exported from Echoview with their corresponding centroid latitude, centroid longitude, centroid depth, and geometries (thickness, length, area, and perimeter). These data were then read into R (version 3.5) and exported as a shape file. The shape file was imported to ArcGIS Pro version 2.4.0 with the ‘Feature to 3D by Attribute’ geoprocessing tool within the ‘3D Analyst’ toolbox and set to the appropriate 3D Symbology where yellow shapes represent fish schools. Sphere height is proportional to the corrected fish school thickness. Sphere width is proportional to the corrected fish school length. Fish school depth is represented in the z-dimension. <br><br>The multibeam_MBTarget3D feature dataset displays fish targets within fish schools acoustically-detected around the shipwreck Hutton offshore of North Carolina, USA. These acoustic data were collected aboard the NOAA Ship Nancy Foster using a Reson 7125 multibeam echo sounder during mission NF-16-09. The survey design included perpendicular along-shipwreck and across-shipwreck survey lines. Following data collection, fish were detected as multibeam targets in Echoview Software using a multibeam target detection algorithm. Fish targets were exported from Echoview by multibeam ping. For each ping, fish target values, including the target range, mean, major axis angle, and minor axis angle were provided. These fish target data were then read into R (version 3.5) and trigonometric calculations were performed to calculate the position of each target (latitude, longitude, depth). These processed data with a latitude, longitude, and depth for each target were exported from R as a shape file. The shape file was imported to ArcGIS Pro version 2.4.0 with the ‘Feature to 3D by Attribute’ geoprocessing tool within the ‘3D Analyst’ toolbox and set to the appropriate 3D Symbology where white spheres represent fish targets. Sphere size is standard and does not vary by attribute. Fish target depth is represented in the z-dimension.<br>These fish targets from wide-angle multibeam convey the extent of fish schools that were detected as a smaller slice with the narrow-beam angle split beam echo sounder. <br><br>The ground layers display bathymetry elevation of shipwrecks offshore of North Carolina, USA.<br>The bathymetric data was collected aboard the NOAA Ship Nancy Foster using a Reson 7125 multibeam echo sounder during mission NF-16-09 and NF-18-06. <br><br>The imagery layers display bathymetry imagery of shipwrecks offshore of North Carolina, USA. The bathymetric data was collected aboard the NOAA Ship Nancy Foster using a Reson 7125 multibeam echo sounder during mission NF-16-09 and NF-18-06. The data is displayed stretched RGB (blue-deep to red-shallow) color scale to best capture the range of depths within the survey area.</td>
</tr>
